  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nThe central idea is to improve the current heuristic optimization by exploring coarse-to-fine resolutions with multiple starting points, enhanced gradient-based methods, and more sophisticated local searches. The bottleneck is the high dimensionality of the optimization space, which easily traps previous methods in local optima. We address this by first optimizing at low resolution with coarse-grained periodic patterns and gradient methods, then progressively moving to higher resolutions with more detailed optimization and refinement. This leverages the intuition that optimal overlaps are often achieved by functions with high \"spectral\" smoothness or \"aperiodic\" qualities, i.e., avoiding early periodicities.\n\nMathematical specification:\nThe problem is to minimize:\n  C = max\u2096 \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx\nsubject to h(x) \u2208 [0,1] and \u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1.\n\nWe discretize [0,2] into N points, with spacing dx = 2/N. Then:\n  h: {0, 1, ... ,N-1} \u2192 [0,1] such that sum(h) * dx = 1 (approximated by sum(h) = N/2 for large N).\n\nThe discrete objective is:\n  C_discrete = max\u2096 { dx * max\u209c [ (h(t) \u2297 (1 - h(t+k)))_{active} ] }\nwhere \u2297 denotes convolution in the discrete space, and the max is taken over valid lags k (ensuring t+k within [0,N-1]).\n\nConstraints:\n  0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n  sum(h) = N/2 (exactly in discrete realization)\n\nWe treat the discrete problem using:\n- Continuous relaxation: replace h[i] with continuous variables (0,1)\n- Objective as negative exponentiated cumulative function to enforce sparsity\n- Constraints via penalty\n\nImplementation procedure:\n\n1. Initialization Phase:\n   a. Validate and adapt `initial_h_values` (if available/possible) for multiple n_points.\n   b. For a broad range of n_points (128, 256, 512, 1024, 2048):\n       i. If initial_h_values available: scale and shift to [0,1] with sum(h)*dx=1\n       ii. Otherwise generate uniform [0,1] vector with integral=1\n   c. Evaluate with original task objective\n   d. Cache past best solution\n\n2. Coarse-Grained Pattern Generation:\n   a. For each resolution (starting from smallest):\n       i. Generate periodic patterns with varying periods and offsets\n       ii. Smooth/reshape to enforce integral=1\n\n3. Gradient-Based Optimization:\n   a. For candidate pattern set:\n        Gradient scheme (Adam optimizer with strong decoupling)\n        Objective: minimize max\u2096 autocorrelation(dx*k)\n        Variable smoothing via hinge penalties\n        Restorative projection onto [0,1] and integral constraint\n\n4. Progressive Resolution Refinement:\n   a. From best found at level L, upsample by 2 and reoptimize\n   b. Use interpolation to maintain continuity +1 constraint enforcement\n   c. Staged refinement: low-pass then detail optimization\n\n5. Local Search & Pseudofractal Refinement:\n   a. Branch-and-bound method with geometric descent\n   b. Variable-step binary/disk optimization (Shuffled Frog Leaping)\n   c. Check against greedy short-cut geometries\n\n6. Insurance Policy:\n   a. Maintain persistent list of past bests\n   b. If stuck in local optimum, roll back\n   c. Monotonic improvement guarantees\n\nSearch choices and useful variations:\n\n1. Configuration: 10 base patterns, 8 resolution levels, 3 optimization runs per level\n2. Pattern diversity: \n   - Steps: 3, 4, 5 (higher step counts signal more precision)\n   - Offset range: 0.125 to 0.875 dx units\n3. Optimization balance:\n   - Use high step count for standard optimization but lower for faster zones\n   - Base parameters: learning_rate=0.85, batch_size=512, patience=64\n   - Exponentiation win-constraint factor: 400 -> if not improving, perturb w/ mutual exclusion\n\nComputebudget and robustness:\n- Time(type): Primarily optimization wall-clock\n- Per-phase:.\n  Coarse: 80 seconds @ level 0\n  Upsample: 40 seconds per level\n  Refine: 40 seconds (final stage)\n- Early interruption:\n   In cross-validation step: if plateaued, skip verification loops\n   Monitor best solution improvement rate: \u22650.05 per hour -> scale down effort\n- Solution formatality:\n   - Input: n_points (large), current_best_solution \u2192 int\n   - Output: best_h (float), new_c5, n_points \u2192 (best_h, new_c5, n_points)\n   - Requires: candidate solution to pass feasibility test\n\nValidation and return contract:\n  - Full forward evaluation via task objective\n  - Tolerance: must fully enforce 0<=h[i]<=1 and normalization\n  - Comparison metric: numerically stable full autocorrelation max\n  - Return only is solution is at least 1e-6 better than known\n  - Insurance: return when budget > (95% completion point + 1min)",
